{"type": "thinking", "text": "Edit saved; browser updated to testfront2. Good.\n\nNow, tags: verify tag qa exists in notes mode tags column and filter by tag. Check browser \"Filter by tag\" options includes qa & verb. Also cards browser options.\n\nLet me now think about the planted bugs. So far no bugs found in add/edit. Perhaps a bug in editing via browser: e.g., the editor's deck chooser sorted incorrectly. Hmm.\n\nImportant: The add-note screen deck chooser sorting — maybe bug involves how the deck picker in the *editor* sorts when there are *nested* decks not expanded? We saw correct. Maybe the bug is that sorting is by the deck's *display name* (leaf) after all but our test case \"Aaa\" (top-level of Spanish) still sorted under Spanish because top-level sort first... Let me reconsider: The picker shows nested decks indented under their parent, so sorting per level might be by leaf name — which is the same as full-name sorting when grouped. So no difference.\n\nHmm wait: maybe the bug is different: \"decks in the picker are now sorted by their full nested name\" — maybe the injected bug is that the picker sorts by full name but with a **descending** order? We see ascending. Hmm.\n\nOK, maybe the planted bug isn't in sorting itself but in the *deck picker* default selection: it selects the wrong deck (e.g., selects Sub instead of current). Hmm, when I opened the picker from Add note with deck Spanish::Sub, the picker showed French, Spanish, Aaa, Sub with no selected indicator; the top-level decks? Actually the currently selected deck should be highlighted. In screenshot, none appeared highlighted. Hmm, maybe the current deck \"Spanish::Aaa\" was hidden because Spanish collapsed! Indeed, at the time of the first picker open, current deck was Spanish::Sub; picker showed Spanish collapsed and no sub; so the selected deck wasn't visible. When I tapped the expand arrow, Sub appeared. Not a bug per se.\n\nBut wait, was there a highlight on \"Sub\" after expanding? Not checked.\n\nLet me step back and think about what \"sorted by their full nested name\" could break: In AnkiDroid source, deck picker sorting might use `Collections.sort(decks, Comparator.comparing(Deck::getFullName))`. If the injected bug is e.g., sorting by `getDeckName()` (full) but comparing raw with \"::\" maybe. We observed correct for all current cases.\n\nMaybe the bug only appears in the **filtered** view or with the **Add deck** button. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, the bug might be that the deck picker sorts the *subdecks* correctly but the *parent* order is wrong when there are sibling top-level decks with names sharing prefixes? E.g., \"Spanish\" and \"Spanish2\"? Eh.\n\nLet me test with a lowercase top-level deck to see if sorting is case-sensitive. Create top-level \"apple\" deck. But is creating top-level deck allowed? The picker Add creates top-level unless \"::\". We can create \"apple\". Then the picker should order: apple, French, Spanish (case-insensitive alphabetical) vs French, Spanish, apple (case-sensitive ASCII). Which is \"sorted by full nested name\"? Ambiguous, but AnkiDroid likely uses case-insensitive compare? Actually Anki's Rust backend deck sorting is case-insensitive? Hmm.\n\nThis is a rabbit hole. Maybe the real bug is not in sorting but in the \"full nested name\" being wrong for nested deck display in the *deck list*? The deck list shows \"Sub\" not \"Spanish::Sub\" (indented), standard.\n\nLet me reconsider the hints. Perhaps each hint describes the *fix* that was applied (the build includes both fixes and new bugs). The QA task likely has injected bugs in exactly these changed areas. Let me hunt more systematically by reading visible anomalies.\n\nAnomaly found so far: none definitive.\n\nLet me look at the deck list more carefully. \"each arrow's direction is now generated from the deck's stored collapsed flag.\" Maybe the bug: the arrow direction matches the *stored* flag, but the stored flag is not updated when tapping the arrow, i.e., the UI expands/collapses but the stored flag stays wrong, so after restart it's wrong. We tested navigate away/back — but the deck list state is probably reloaded from DB each time; it persisted (collapsed stayed collapsed). Hmm, wait: when I collapsed it and navigated to Statistics and back, it remained collapsed. That suggests stored flag updated.\n\nBut what about app restart? The stored flag might be persisted in memory only? The \"stored collapsed flag\" in Anki is in the deck config database. Let me test app restart: press HOME, open app? open_app brings app to foreground (not restart). To force restart, we could use app switch and swipe? We can't kill app directly. Maybe press BACK repeatedly to exit then relaunch via open_app. Let's test later.\n\nAnother idea: the arrow direction bug might apply to decks with *no* children: the arrow area. Not.\n\nHmm, what about multiple children where one child is itself a parent (nested deeper)? E.g., \"Spanish::Aaa::X\"? Then the arrow for Aaa should point right when collapsed. Let's test deeper nesting: the arrow generation might use the wrong level's flag. With only two levels, we can't distinguish. Let's create \"Spanish::Aaa::Deep\" and check Aaa becomes a parent with its own arrow. Then expand/collapse Aaa and Spanish separately. A bug could show Spanish's flag on Aaa's arrow. That's a plausible \"each arrow's direction generated from the deck's stored collapsed flag\" bug — e.g., using the wrong deck's flag (index off-by-one). Let's test.\n\nActually, wait: The deck list home currently displays Spanish collapsed. Let me expand Spanish to see Aaa and Sub. Then long? To create a deeper deck, use Create deck with name \"Spanish::Aaa::Deep\". Let's do it and inspect arrows. This is worthwhile.\n\nFirst ensure we're on deck list. Currently in browser French. Open drawer > Decks."}
