{"type": "thinking", "text": "Arrows correct: Spanish down, Aaa right. Interesting: \"Deep\" hidden because Aaa collapsed. All good.\n\nSo where's the injected deck-arrow bug? Maybe with the *default* collapsed state? Hmm. Or maybe arrow direction is correct but *tap target* wrong? E.g., tapping the arrow of Aaa toggles Spanish instead? We tapped Aaa's arrow and it expanded Aaa (Deep appeared) while Spanish stayed expanded. So correct.\n\nHmm, maybe the bug is that the arrow is rendered from the flag of the *deck whose row it's on* but \"stored collapsed flag\" gets out of sync when you collapse a parent and then expand a child? Let me test subtle sequences:\n- Collapse Spanish (children hidden), then expand Spanish. Aaa should still be collapsed (flag persisted separately). That's what we saw earlier: after re-expanding Spanish, Aaa showed \"Expand\" (collapsed) — correct.\n- What if you collapse Aaa while Spanish collapsed? Not possible (hidden).\n\nLet me test long-press on deck? Not needed.\n\nHmm. Maybe the deck list bug is about the arrow direction when the deck is *empty of cards* but has subdecks. Aaa has 0 cards but subdeck Deep. Arrow works.\n\nWait, maybe there's a known AnkiDroid quirk: deck list collapse arrow is hidden when the deck and all its children have no cards? Not.\n\nLet me reconsider the task design. It's likely a scripted app with monkey-injected bug into one specific code path. The \"changed in this build\" list gives areas. Each area probably has exactly one injected bug. We need to find 3 (or maybe fewer). So far I found none, which means I'm missing subtle ones.\n\nLet me re-examine the Cards/Notes toggle more carefully. The hint \"each mode's display settings are now read from one shared place.\" This could be interpreted as: previously each mode had its own display settings; now one shared place. The injected bug may be that the \"Cards\" and \"Notes\" modes now share the *columns* settings — but we observed separate columns... Wait, did we? Initially Cards columns: Sort Field, Card Type, Due, Deck. Notes: Sort Field, Note Type, Cards, Tags. These are the *default* column sets. If both modes read from \"one shared place\", maybe they'd show the same columns; they don't. So not columns.\n\nWhat about the \"truncate content\" and \"ignore accents\" checkboxes — those are shared (we observed). In real AnkiDroid, are they shared? Let me actually check AnkiDroid documentation/source from memory. Searching memory: `Prefs.BROWSER_TRUNCATE_CONTENT = \"browserTruncateContent\"`, and in BrowserOptions:\n\n```java\nmTruncateCheckBox = view.findViewById(R.id.truncate_check_box);\n...\nmPreferences.getBoolean(Prefs.BROWSER_TRUNCATE_CONTENT, false)\n```\n\nHmm I really think it's global. But there's also `Prefs.BROWSER_IGNORE_ACCENTS_IN_SEARCH` global.\n\nSo maybe the injected bug is exactly that these are now shared — i.e., originally AnkiDroid might have separate per-mode prefs like `BROWSER_TRUNCATE_CONTENT_CARDS/NOTES`? Hmm, not that I recall.\n\nWait, actually! I recall a recent AnkiDroid change: \"Browser: cards/notes mode now has separate truncate content setting\"? Hmm. In AnkiDroid 2.17 there was a settings revamp. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"display settings\" refers to the **columns** and the bug is more subtle than the column list: e.g., after switching modes, the sort order is read from the shared (other mode's) place. Let me test sort indices.\n\nCard mode columns: [Sort Field, Card Type, Due, Deck]. Notes columns: [Sort Field, Note Type, Cards, Tags]. Suppose sorting preference stores the column index. When in Notes mode, choosing sort by \"Tags\" (index 3) is stored. Then switching to Cards mode, the browser reads the shared sort index (3) and sorts by \"Deck\" in cards mode (index 3), instead of by its own stored sort. Hmm, but that's actually how AnkiDroid might work: sort is per mode with separate prefs, or the sort is by a \"sort type\" enum.\n\nLet me test: In Notes mode, click header \"Tags\" to sort by tags ascending. Then switch to Cards and observe what column is sorted (header arrow). If the header arrow appears on the wrong column or order is odd, that's a bug. Also test \"Change display order\" dialog after switching: which radio is checked? If it shows the shared sort (Tags) while in Cards mode, that's a clear bug.\n\nLet's do that. Exit deck list to browser. Drawer > Card browser. Currently browser French cards mode. Switch to Notes via options (Save). Then tap Tags header. Then switch back to Cards via options, and look at Change display order.\n\nNote: Clicks on headers sort. Let's do it."}
