{"type": "thinking", "text": "Cards mode sort options include Cards-mode columns: Sort Field, Card Type, Due, Deck, Tags, Ease, Card Modified, Created, Note Modified, Interval. Correct, and Sort Field asc checked. Separate sort per mode. Fine.\n\nClose overlay with BACK. Notes sort remains Tags desc; that's a setting change I made. I should restore Notes sort to Sort Field asc. Do that at the end (switch to notes, choose Sort Field asc). Let's remember.\n\nAt this point I haven't found bugs. Let me broaden to browser options: Filter by tag, filter marked/suspended, preview, select all, find and replace. These are part of \"card browser and its options\". Maybe the injected bug is in a filter or \"Select all\".\n\nAlso the deck list: maybe options menu items (Check database, Create backup...). And the deck list item counts. And add/edit notes: type spinner, tags, cards button, preview, undo, etc.\n\nLet me systematically sweep the browser's options:\n- Filter marked: no marked cards → should show 0 cards shown or \"no cards matched\"? Test.\n- Filter suspended: none suspended → empty.\n- Filter by tag: shows tags (verb, qa) and selecting filters.\n- Filter by flag: none flagged.\n- Preview: works.\n- Select all: selects all rows; then actions (change deck, etc.).\n- Find and replace.\n\nBut the \"changed\" hints are the likely bug locations. Let me reconsider each hint and think about what bug could be *observable* with the given starting data:\n\nHint 1: add note deck chooser sorted by full nested name. With starting data (no nested decks), deck order French, Spanish. If sorting was broken (e.g., case-insensitive vs not), no difference. So the bug might be that the sort is reversed? French, Spanish is ascending. Hmm, unless the *intended* order in the picker should be by... no, ascending.\n\nWait wait. Maybe the bug is that the picker sorts decks by full nested name but should place the **currently selected deck** first or something? No.\n\nHmm, actually, maybe the sort bug only appears because the picker uses the *deck IDs* not names? With starting data, deck order in DB is French then Spanish (creation order). Ascending alphabetical also French, Spanish. So no way to distinguish with starting data! Unless we add decks. That's why I added nested decks. We created Spanish::Aaa and it sorted correctly. Hmm, so no bug.\n\nUnless... the sort should be *case-insensitive*, and our test decks are capitalized. Let me create \"Spanish::aaa\" lowercase. Full-name sort: \"Spanish::Aaa\" vs \"Spanish::aaa\": case-sensitive ASCII puts \"Aaa\" before \"aaa\"; case-insensitive comparisons maybe tie. Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the bug is that the sort uses the **full name including the deck's *display name* of parent** but deck names are raw including separators. Not.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the actual AnkiDroid behavior for the deck picker is sorted by name, and the injected bug is *not* about order but about the **path displayed**: e.g., nested deck shown as \"Aaa\" instead of full \"Spanish::Aaa\" (though indentation). Hmm, the change says \"decks in the picker are now sorted by their full nested name\" — about sorting only.\n\nMaybe the bug: sorting includes only the *children of the selected deck*? Hmm.\n\nHmm, let me look at the deck picker right now with nested decks, in the *browser* deck selector (not add note): it showed \"All decks\", French, Spanish collapsed. Alphabetical. Fine.\n\nWhat if the parent decks are sorted by full name but subdecks are not inserted right after parents when there are multiple top-level decks with children? e.g., decks: French (has child \"French::Z\"), Spanish (has child). Picker should show French, French::Z, Spanish, Spanish::Z. Let's test: add a subdeck under French: \"French::A\". Then picker should show French, A under it, Spanish... This is standard. Current implementation appears tree-based, so fine.\n\nMaybe the sort bug is in the **\"Filter decks\"** search results? Eh.\n\nLet me try a different tack: maybe the injected bugs are not all in the hinted features; the hints are red herrings describing changes but the bugs could be elsewhere. The task says \"Changed in this build\" — likely where bugs are. But maybe only one or two of the changes contain bugs.\n\nLet me test the Add note deck chooser more: after selecting a deck, does the note save to that deck? We saved to French and it worked.\n\nWhat about creating a note in a **subdeck** and checking the deck counts/parent counts? Could expose deck-related bug (parent count should aggregate). But we shouldn't add data unnecessarily; we already have test note; could move/delete.\n\nWhat about editing a note's deck via the editor? Change deck to Spanish::Sub and save; verify browser shows move. That's \"editing notes\" scope. Could reveal a bug in deck chooser sorting/selection. But that changes data; can move back.\n\nActually, here's a thought: the Add note deck chooser sort bug might be that after creating nested decks, the *Add note editor's* deck dropdown opened with the current deck \"Spanish::Aaa\" but the picker collapsed Spanish hiding the selection. That's UX but not a bug.\n\nLet me test the \"Add note\" deck chooser persistence: select Spanish, save the note, then open Add note again — the deck should default to the last used deck (Spanish). Typically AnkiDroid remembers. We saw defaults: first Add after creating Sub → Sub; after creating Aaa → Aaa. That's the \"last created deck\" behavior, consistent.\n\nHmm.\n\nLet me examine the deck list overflow \"Check\" (Check database) maybe not relevant.\n\nMaybe the bug in the deck list is about the *counts on parent decks*: Spanish shows 4, and if I move a note into Spanish::Sub, Spanish should still show 5 (aggregated) while Sub shows 1. Add/edit tests could cover. But the hint is about arrows.\n\nWait, maybe the arrow bug: The arrow direction is generated from the deck's stored collapsed flag — but the *stored flag was changed by our collapsing*, and the arrow direction updates. Fine. What if the deck is collapsed by default when created (as we saw), whereas in real AnkiDroid newly created subdecks are *expanded* by default? The default collapsed flag for a new deck might be true (collapsed) or false. In Anki/AnkiDroid, new decks are expanded? Hmm, the *parent* is expanded to show the new child? I believe when you create a subdeck, AnkiDroid expands the parent automatically (or remembers). But is the \"stored collapsed flag\" semantics inverted at creation? If the injected bug is that the flag is stored inverted, then arrow direction would be wrong relative to actual expansion state... but default collapsed a new parent is plausible (AnkiDroid remembers collapsed state; new decks might default to expanded false). Not a strong bug.\n\nHmm. Let me look at the deck list screen with a parent that has cards and children: the parent row shows chevron at x=63 and the counts layout at right. Wait — in the screenshot with Spanish expanded, the parent row's counts \"4 0 0\" is at the right, fine. But notice the \"Sub\" row: it's highlighted grey (selected?) because it was the current deck. That's fine.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtle thing: In the deck list, the collapse arrow for Spanish is at x=63 while for Aaa at x=110. In the screenshot, the Spanish row's chevron is left of \"Spanish\", Aaa's chevron indented. Good.\n\nOK, let me now consider the possibility that all three \"changed\" hints are things to verify, and the actual injected bugs are somewhere obvious I haven't hit yet, e.g.:\n- Browser: editing note via browser, \"Cards\" button in editor (shows card template) maybe broken.\n- Add note: \"Cards: Card 1\" button behavior.\n- Deck list: long-press context menu (rename/delete/export).\n\nBut the task said \"sweep these areas\" with a colleague asking specifically about card browser and options, deck list, adding/editing notes. It's a broad sweep; bugs may be anywhere in these areas, not necessarily the