{"type": "thinking", "text": "Notes mode with tag filter: 1 note shown, comer with verb tag. Columns notes-specific correct.\n\nNote: The Notes sort I set earlier was Tags Z-A. With one result no effect.\n\nNow let's test \"Preview\" on comer. And \"Select all\" with filter.\n\nActually, more promising: let's examine the **\"Select all\"** with filter and the **browser menu actions**. But time.\n\nLet me reconsider the hints once more. Maybe I'm missing that the \"Add note deck chooser\" bug is about ordering when the *deck picker* is opened from the **Add note** screen vs the **browser**. We saw add-note picker: French, Spanish, [Aaa, Sub]. Browser picker: All decks, French, Spanish, [Aaa,...]. Both sorted.\n\nWait, maybe the bug is this: the deck picker shows subdecks sorted correctly, BUT the **deck list home** (deck list) sort is broken? We saw French, Spanish, Aaa, Sub, Deep? Hmm, deck list home shows French then Spanish (with children Aaa, Sub). Aaa has Deep hidden. If I expand Aaa, order Aaa, Deep, Sub? Earlier after expanding both: Spanish, Aaa, Deep, Sub. Full-name order: Spanish::Aaa, Spanish::Aaa::Deep, Spanish::Sub. Correct tree order.\n\nHmm.\n\nLet me try the case-sensitive sort test to look for a bug in the deck picker. Create top-level deck \"apple\" (lowercase). Then see picker order. If order is \"French, Spanish, apple\" → case-sensitive ASCII, likely a bug per \"sorted by full nested name\" (should be locale-aware \"apple, French, Spanish\"). But is that really the injected bug? Could be! A common injected bug is using `String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER` vs natural ordering. Hmm.\n\nBut wait, the change says \"sorted by their full nested name\" — the emphasis \"full nested name\" suggests the original bug was sorting by the *leaf* name (e.g., \"Sub\" sorted under S instead of under Spanish). Our test with Aaa showed it sorted under Spanish (indented), not top-level. If sorted by leaf name in a flat list, \"Aaa\" would appear at top-level between French? Actually in a tree UI, leaf-name sorting per level looks identical to full-name sorting. Hmm, unless the picker is a flat list without tree? It's a tree with indentation, so leaf name sorting per level is effectively the same for tree order. So \"full nested name\" matters when... the picker sorts all decks as a flat list but displays with indentation? No.\n\nWait, maybe in AnkiDroid's deck picker, subdecks are only shown under expanded parents, and sorting is applied to the whole list; full name sort vs leaf name sort gives the same order within each parent. Hmm, so indistinguishable.\n\nUnless the injected bug caused the *top-level* order to be affected by children's names, e.g., sorting the flat list by full name but the tree builder uses the sorted flat list without grouping — that could place children wrongly. But we saw correct grouping.\n\nI wonder if the picker bug appears with a deck whose name is a *prefix* of another top-level deck: e.g., \"Spanish\" and \"Spanish 2\"? Full-name lexicographic: \"Spanish\" < \"Spanish 2\" (< \"Spanish::Aaa\" because space (32) < ':' (58)). Hmm interesting! With full nested name sorting, \"Spanish 2\" would come BEFORE \"Spanish::Aaa\" if comparing raw strings, because space < colon. But tree grouping would keep subdecks under Spanish. Our tests only had \"Spanish::Aaa\" and \"Spanish::Sub\". So no insight.\n\nHmm, the \"::\" separator sorts after most ASCII letters? ':' is 58, space 32, digits 48, letters 65+. So a deck \"Spanish Aaa\" (top-level) sorts before \"Spanish::Sub\". Tree display still groups Spanish::Sub under Spanish. So the full-name sort ordering among *top-level* decks is unaffected by children. So the only visible effect of \"sorted by full nested name\" is within a parent's child list: e.g., children \"Aaa\" and \"Sub\". Sorting by leaf vs full is the same within the same parent.\n\nTherefore the change is essentially unobservable unless... the leaf names include the parent prefix? Hmm.\n\nUnless the picker is a **flat list** (not tree) in some mode, e.g., when filtering/searching. Let's test \"Filter decks\" search: type \"s\" and see all decks including \"Spanish::Aaa\" displayed as full names? Let's look. In the browser or add-note picker, tap Filter decks [1], type \"S\". This might show flat results like \"Spanish\", \"Spanish::Aaa\", \"Spanish::Sub\". If sorting is by leaf name, \"Aaa\" might come first. Hmm! Actually filter results would show full names probably. Let's test.\n\nLet me clear the search filter first (clear query [2]) to exit search mode. Then open deck picker > Filter decks.\n\nActually I'm in Notes mode in search. Clear query [2]."}
