al deck filter was Default, not Spanish! Ugh. The first time we opened the browser it showed \"Default | 0 cards shown\". Then I selected Spanish. So to restore exactly, I'd set deck filter Default. But the \"Default\" deck isn't in the picker! It showed All decks, French, Spanish only. Hmm, so \"Default\" deck wasn't listed but was the initial filter. Interesting.\n\nWait, how did the browser initially have \"Default\" while the picker doesn't show it? Because the \"Default\" deck might be hidden in the picker (empty deck) but can be the toolbar deck. Hmm. Anyway, restoring it may not be possible via picker. Maybe select \"All decks\" and accept. It's a browser filter state; not critical data.\n\nThe key data to restore: delete test note, test decks, and ideally qa tag. Settings: restore notes sort to Sort Field asc, browser mode Cards, truncate off (it is), notes columns default (restored).\n\nBut before cleanup, I still need to decide whether there are bugs. I have found none so far. Let me do a more targeted examination of the three changed behaviors with adversarial tests:\n\nA. Add note deck chooser sorting — maybe the bug is that the picker is sorted by full nested name **case-sensitively**, breaking for mixed-case names. Let me test by creating a lowercase deck? The precondition data has no lowercase. But the injected bug might be undetectable with the given data unless we add decks. The QA instruction says use the starting data; adding decks is \"testing requires\" arguably. We already added decks and found correct alphabetical. To test case sensitivity, create top-level \"apple\". The expected sort by full name in AnkiDroid: I believe Anki's deck tree sorts case-insensitively (Rust backend `natural_compare`). If picker shows French, Spanish, apple then it's case-sensitive → bug. Let's test this since it's cheap: create top-level \"apple\" via picker Add, observe position, then delete.\n\nB. Deck arrows — maybe bug with **deck name starting with lowercase**? no.\n\nC. Cards/notes toggle — maybe shared settings bug is that **truncate content is shared** and the expected behavior is per-mode. Hmm, I could look for evidence in the UI: In the options dialog, the \"Truncate content\" section title is a single checkbox. If it were per-mode, AnkiDroid would likely still show one checkbox that applies to the current mode and persists separately. There's no way to tell from UI.\n\nWait, maybe there's a *second* settings section for the other mode? No.\n\nHmm, let me look at the app's Settings screen for browser-related prefs. Maybe the app exposes \"Browser\" settings with separate notes/cards? Unlikely.\n\nLet me test A quickly. We're in the browser deck picker; cancel it, go to Add note, use picker Add to create \"apple\". Actually we can use the deck list FAB > Create deck. Let's use the browser picker Add (action_add_deck) to create \"apple\"; it defaults to top-level. Then reopen picker/see order. Then delete via long-press in deck list.\n\nActually, creating top-level \"apple\" might disturb sorting comparisons with \"French\"/\"Spanish\". Let's do it and observe. Then delete.\n\nCancel picker [10], open drawer > Decks, FAB > Create deck."}
