{"type": "thinking", "text": "Cancel reverted truncate (unchecked). Good.\n\nHmm. So the browser options behave correctly. I'm increasingly doubting there are obvious bugs in these areas. Let me approach more scientifically: The \"changed in this build\" hints likely point to **specific code paths**, and the injected bug likely causes a **visible discrepancy**. For the cards/notes toggle, the phrase \"each mode's display settings are now read from one shared place\" could mean the *columns* are read from one shared place in the sense of a `BrowserColumns` instance. A bug might be: **the cards mode and notes mode share the same column configuration when the app is freshly installed, and changing the columns in one mode changes the other** — but we tested and it didn't. Unless our test (adding Deck to Notes) didn't actually change cards *because the app reads columns per mode for display but the manage screen writes shared*? We tested both: after adding Deck to Notes, cards mode remained 4 columns. So separate.\n\nMaybe the bug is the opposite: **the display settings for each mode are supposed to be independent, but the *sort order* is shared** — tested, separate.\n\nMaybe **the mode preference itself** is shared with something: e.g., the \"Cards/Notes\" mode preference is stored in the same pref key as \"show notes\" and toggling changes... no.\n\nHmm, maybe the bug is about **the display *row height/truncate* applying the wrong mode's setting at render time**: The browser reads truncate setting from one shared place, but the *dialog* writes to a per-mode place? If the stored truncate value is per-mode but the browser renders from a single pref... We could test: set truncate ON in Notes, see the notes list rows truncate (with short content can't tell), then switch to Cards: rows should be untruncated. With short rows, no visible difference. We could create a long field to see 3-line truncation, but that's a lot of work and the exact behavior (row height) is testable via screenshot? The list rows are single-line here anyway. Hmm.\n\nActually, we can test truncate visibly with a **long note**: create a note with long front (multi-line) in French; set truncate ON and observe row height limited to 3 lines; switch mode and see if cards mode truncated/untruncated. But content-based truncation in cards mode applies to the card question/answer content, which for Basic is {{Front}}. A long front would show long row. This could reveal cross-mode leakage. But is truncate a per-mode setting in the actual app? If not, no bug.\n\nLet me step back and consider that maybe the bug is not in the toggle settings but in the **Cards/Notes mode itself not being saved to the collection**, causing the *browser count/summary* to be wrong. Hmm.\n\nActually — wait! I just remembered something about AnkiDroid: In the browser, the \"Cards\" vs \"Notes\" mode determines the **row model**, and the **\"Notes\" mode shows one row per note**. The hint \"each mode's display settings are now read from one shared place\" — maybe it refers to `mBrowserMode` being read from `AnkiDroidApp.getSharedPrefs()`. The injected bug could be that switching to Notes mode shows the wrong data model: e.g., Notes mode lists *cards* (duplicates for multi-card notes) or Cards mode lists *notes*. Our notes each have 1 card, so the row counts are identical (4 notes, 4 cards) — we can't tell! Ahh, that's a key insight: all our notes have a single card (Basic). So Cards vs Notes mode may be indistinguishable by count/columns? But the columns differ (Card Type vs Note Type), so we can tell mode via headers. The data rows: Notes mode rows show tags; Cards mode rows show Due. They differ. So mode is right.\n\nBut maybe the *underlying query* is shared: e.g., in Notes mode the row for a multi-card note appears once (correct) vs cards twice. With single-card notes, can't test. To test, we'd need a note type with 2 cards (e.g., Basic with reverse card) — we could create a \"Basic (and reversed card)\" note. But notes types: the spinner offers Basic only? Let's check the note type spinner: it might have \"Basic\", \"Basic (and reversed card)\", \"Basic (optional reversed card)\", \"Cloze\". If we add a reversed note, then check counts in Cards vs Notes mode: Cards should show 2 cards, Notes 1 note. That's a good test of the mode toggle's data reading! The hint \"display settings read from one shared place\" might relate to the *rows* — but more likely the mode's row type.\n\nLet's test this: In the Add note screen, tap Type spinner to see available note types. Precondition only Basic notes exist, but note types exist by default (Basic, Basic (and reversed card), Cloze). Creating a note of another type adds data; we can delete it after. But it modifies notes types usage; deleting the note removes it. We already need cleanup. This could reveal a cards/notes mode bug: e.g., Notes mode shows 2 rows for the reversed note (if it queries cards) or Cards mode shows 1 row (if it queries notes).\n\nThis is worth doing. Let's first close the options dialog (Cancel), then Add note, choose \"Basic (and reversed card)\", fill fields, save, then check browser counts in Cards vs Notes.\n\nBut wait, note: the deck is \"apple\" now (last created). I'll add to apple, then delete note and deck. Fine. Actually, better add to French? The deck picker sorting is fine. Let me add to apple and clean up.\n\nHmm, but if the note type \"Basic (and reversed card)\" doesn't exist, no harm.\n\nLet's do it: Cancel options [13]. Then Add via browser toolbar [2]. Type spinner."}
